HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) is a type of web application vulnerability that allows an attacker to bypass input validation and Web Application Firewall (WAF) rules. This occurs when a web application uses the same parameter name across multiple query strings in a URL.
For example, consider the following URLs:
http://example.com/search?q=apple
http://example.com/search?q=banana
http://example.com/search?q=apple&q=banana
In the first two URLs, the parameter name is q. In the third URL, the parameter name q is repeated. This is an example of HTTP Parameter Pollution.
An attacker can exploit HPP by repeating a parameter name in a URL. This allows the attacker to bypass input validation and WAF rules. For example, consider the following URLs:
http://example.com/search?q=apple
http://example.com/search?q=banana
http://example.com/search?q=apple&q=banana
The first two URLs are legitimate. The third URL is not legitimate, but it is not blocked by the WAF because the parameter name q is repeated. The attacker can use the third URL to exploit the web application.
To prevent HPP, you should validate each parameter name individually. You can do this by using a parameter name whitelist or by checking each parameter name against a list of expected names.
You can also prevent HPP by using a WAF that is designed to detect and prevent HPP. These WAFs can be configured to block requests that contain repeated parameter names.
